Key Points to Remember

. Checked exceptions have rules
If the parent method throws a checked exception, the child method must follow the same or stricter rules. It can throw
the same or more specific, but not broader exceptions.
. Runtime exceptions are flexible
Runtime exceptions (like NullPointerException ) can be added or removed freely in the child class.
. You can remove exceptions safely
If your child class can handle things internally, you don't have to throw the exception again. But only do this if it's safe
and makes sense.
. Be specific, not general
Prefer using specific exception types. Avoid catching or throwing general Exception unless truly necessary.
Common Interview Questions & Answers

Q1: Can a child class throw a broader exception than its parent method?
Answer: No. A child class cannot throw a broader (more general) checked exception. It must throw the same or a more
specific one.

Q2: What happens if the parent class throws a checked exception, but the child class does not?
Answer: That's totally fine. The child method can choose not to throw any exception - it just means it's handling the
issue safely on its own.

Q3: Can runtime exceptions be added or removed in the overridden method?
Answer: Yes. Runtime exceptions are unchecked and don't need to be declared. You can add or remove them in the child
class without any compiler errors.


